//! # Types, traits, and patterns
//!
//! `type` declares a record and `enum` declares a closed choice of variants.
//! Both can have generic parameters. There is no `struct` keyword. Fields and
//! variants end with semicolons; commas separate literal values and pattern
//! elements.
//!
//! ```hew
//! type Pair<A, B> {
//!     first: A;
//!     second: B;
//! }
//!
//! trait Label {
//!     fn label(self) -> string;
//! }
//!
//! type Point {
//!     x: i64;
//!     y: i64;
//! }
//!
//! impl Label for Point {
//!     fn label(self) -> string { f"({self.x}, {self.y})" }
//! }
//!
//! enum Shape {
//!     Dot(Point);
//!     Rectangle { width: i64; height: i64; }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let pair = Pair { first: 3, second: "east" };
//!     let shape = Shape.Dot(Point { x: pair.first, y: 4 });
//!     match shape {
//!         .Dot(point) => println(point.label()),
//!         .Rectangle { width, height } => println(width * height),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Record construction is keyed: `Point { y: 2, x: 1 }` is valid, and
//! `Point { ..base, x: 3 }` copies the fields not named explicitly. A binding
//! must be `var` to assign through one of its fields.
//!
//! Variant constructors can use the type name (`Shape.Dot(point)`) or a leading
//! dot when the expected enum type is known (`.Dot(point)`). Match patterns use
//! the same tuple or record shape as their variant.
//!
//! ## Traits and implementations
//!
//! A trait lists required methods and associated types. `impl Trait for Type`
//! supplies that contract; `impl Type` declares inherent methods. Method calls
//! use dot syntax, while associated functions use the type name. Generic bounds
//! use `T: Trait + Other`; associated-type constraints use
//! `Iterator<Item = T>`. Longer constraints can follow the signature in a
//! `where` clause.
//!
//! `dyn Trait` is a runtime trait object. It preserves the methods and ownership
//! capabilities declared by the trait; it is not implicitly cloneable or
//! sendable unless the trait contract establishes those properties.
//!
//! Type aliases use `type Name = ExistingType;`. They give another spelling to
//! the same type rather than creating a new nominal type.
